    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Are you a curious person?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "If you’re often "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "eager",
            "ref": "v-eager"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to learn more about something, then you probably are. But as American researcher Perry Zurn points out, not all curious minds are cut from the same cloth. There are at least three styles of curiosity: “busybodies”, “hunters”, and “dancers.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Busybodies” like to collect bits of information on all kinds of topics. They’re not "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "chasing",
            "ref": "v-chase"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " any certain goal. Rather, they’re just interested in the world around them. “Hunters”, by contrast, go after "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "specific",
            "ref": "v-specific"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " answers. They follow a clear path to reach their goals. “Dancers” jump at new ideas or combine old ones in new ways. They prefer to take the road less travelled."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Zurn and his team went on to see how these curiosity styles play out in real life. They focused on readers of the online encyclopedia Wikipedia. Their research included 482,000 people from 50 countries. For each person, the team looked at how many different topics he or she read about and how closely those topics were connected. For example, did someone look up Pierre Perifel and then read about his new film, "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "The Bad Guys 2"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "? Or did he or she jump from the film director to "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Pu’er tea",
            "zh": "普洱茶"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The results showed that all three types of curious minds exist among today’s internet users. “Busybodies” read widely, but they especially liked sports, food, and the arts. “Hunters” cared more about science and technology. Interestingly, they were more likely to feel "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "stressed",
            "ref": "v-stressed"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " after their curious hunt than the other two types. That’s probably because they had a stronger drive to fill in the blanks in their knowledge."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Zurn says we are each born with a natural curiosity style. However, our style isn’t set in stone. We can develop the other two styles by spending time with teachers or friends who think differently. That’s good news. After all, curiosity works best when all three styles exist in one mind."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
